using static StrataLint.Scribe.DefinitionDsl;

namespace StrataLint.Scribe.Blueprint.D5.S3.Geometry.AlgebraicBoundary;

internal sealed class ConeMinimumBranchDocument : IScribeDocumentDefinition
{
    public DocumentDefinition Create() => DocumentDefinition.Create(ScribeNode.Create(
        "Actual low-degree cone minima, roots and entire closed branches.",
        H("Actual low-degree cone minima, roots and entire closed branches."),
        Blocks(
            Paragraph(Text("For a homogeneous minimum entire-boundary equation, absence of interior zeros follows from the actual compact base. The strictly positive normalization, its normalized interior point and the kernel coordinate equivalence are constructed from the original closed salient full cone. Exact degree and minimum correspondence transfer the minimum equation to the compact body, where an interior zero would yield a lower-degree entire-boundary equation. In the zero-dimensional kernel case every original vector is a scalar multiple of the normalized point, and nonzero homogeneity excludes an interior zero directly. Continuity on the convex original interior then supplies one common sign. The analytic branch argument uses these derived facts. For a point x in C and t<0, x+(-t)e lies in the original interior, so homogeneity excludes q_x(t)=0. Conversely, if x is outside C, the negative-cone ray endpoint gives an actual negative root; on the axis, the root s itself is negative. This yields equality between C and the entire set of points whose q_x roots are all nonnegative. Every interior direction and every original vector are retained. The original interior is exactly the connected component of the polynomial nonzero set containing the direction, and its closure is the original closed cone. Any other nonzero entire-boundary equation of the same minimum total degree is homogeneous: every lower homogeneous component vanishes on the entire frontier and would contradict minimum degree. Subtract a scalar multiple of the selected minimum equation to cancel its value at an interior point. If the difference were nonzero it would again be a minimum equation with an interior zero. Hence the original minimum equation is unique up to a nonzero real scalar. The analytic argument concerns minimum degree at most three. The separate arbitrary-degree minimum_boundary_ideal theorem proves that every polynomial vanishing on the entire original boundary is divisible by this same q, with no degree bound or homogeneity assumption on the comparison equation. Its converse follows by evaluation. Thus q is the actual homogeneous squarefree principal generator of the entire boundary ideal, and its total degree is the original minimum. This identification uses the original carrier transport and the actual finite-degree minimum, rather than defining a new name for a boundary-covering equation. The same host also consumes arbitrary-degree boundary component recovery for the original input equation: the original cone is the closure of the union of all polynomial-complement components lying in its interior. Finiteness and semialgebraicity of these components remain separate obligations; no semialgebraicity conclusion is asserted here.")),
            Paragraph(Text("In a one-dimensional original carrier, the constructed normalization u and normalized interior point b satisfy x=u(x)b for every vector. The cone is exactly u(x) nonnegative and its entire frontier is the original vertex. The polynomial representing u is homogeneous of total degree one. A polynomial vanishes on that entire frontier exactly when this linear polynomial divides it, by the actual hyperplane factorization. Thus the ray case has its actual principal boundary ideal, rather than a boundary-covering surrogate.")),
            Paragraph(Text("For the converse, suppose a homogeneous polynomial is nonzero at an original interior direction and its whole closed nonnegative-root branch is exactly the original cone. At a boundary point where the polynomial were nonzero, the actual line polynomial would have no zero root. Every real root is positive because the point belongs to the branch. Its finite set of roots has a strictly positive common lower bound epsilon, including the case of an empty root set. Translating the point backwards by epsilon times the direction shifts every real root by minus epsilon and therefore stays in the same whole branch. Adding that positive multiple of the interior direction then puts the original boundary point in the interior, a contradiction. Consequently this same polynomial vanishes on the entire original boundary. This uses the genuine whole-branch equality; hyperbolicity plus boundary covering alone does not supply it.")))));
}
